.TH CVEC_CONSTRUCT_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_construct_scoped \- construct a vector destroyed automatically at scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_construct_scoped(v, type);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro declares a vector variable named v that holds elements of the
given type, creates the vector, and adds a cleanup handler that destroys
the vector when control leaves the scope around it. The macro is
cvec_construct(3) plus that cleanup handler, with one more difference:
this macro initializes the hidden type marker variables, which the other
type-dispatching macros read, to NULL, while cvec_construct(3)
deliberately leaves them indeterminate. Everything else that
cvec_construct(3) documents holds here unchanged, including the stop of
the program through ccol_fatal_err() for a failed creation.

The destruction happens on every exit from the scope: the closing brace,
a return, a break, a continue, or a goto out of the block. Because the
macro sets the variable to NULL as it destroys the vector, nothing is
freed twice when you also call cvec_destroy() before the end of the
scope.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v     Name of the vector variable to declare and create
type  Element type, for example int, double or struct foo
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() when an allocation
fails.
The macro is built on __attribute__((cleanup)), so it needs GCC or Clang.
On a toolchain that does not have that attribute, use cvec_construct(3)
with an explicit cvec_destroy().
Because the macro expands to a declaration, it belongs among the
statements of a block and cannot be the body of an if or a loop without
braces.
The vector must not live longer than its scope: do not return v, and do
not store it anywhere that code reads after the end of the block.
When the vector holds pointers to heap buffers, the macro does not free
the elements one by one, so free the element data before you leave the
scope.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void process(void) {
    cvec_construct_scoped(vec, int);
    cvec_push(vec, 42);

    if (cvec_size(vec) != 1) {
        return;   // vec is destroyed here
    }
}                 // and here
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvec_construct (3),
.BR cvector_create (3),
.BR cvector_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_scoped_ptr (3)
